// Chapter XI: Who Stole the Tarts? — Complete (Pages 162–175)
// Starts on Verso (p. 162). Includes White Rabbit Herald + "The Queen of Hearts" (#37 p. 166),
// Trembling Hatter wrap (#38 p. 170), and Fleeing Hatter mid-plate (#39 p. 173).

\use "components/Align.dt"
\use "components/Spacer.dt"
\use "components/Italic.dt"
\use "AliceStyle.dt"

\Component ChapterEleven(
    x: Distance = parent.left,
    y: Distance = parent.top,
    width: Distance = parent.width,
    pagination: Pagination = \Pagination(breakable: true),
    env font: Font,
    env chapter_meta: PageValue,
    env chapter_end_meta: PageValue
) {
    \let ch = \AliceChapterPlain(
        x: x,
        y: y,
        width: width,
        number: "XI",
        title: "WHO STOLE THE TARTS",
        title_punct: "?",
        verso_header: "WHO STOLE",
        recto_header: "THE TARTS?",
        header_lines: 12.0,
        ch_top: 140pt,
        break_before: Break.Page
    ) {
        // ============================================================
        // Pages 162–165: The Courtroom & Jury-Box
        // ============================================================
        \Para {
            T\SmallCaps{HE} King and Queen of Hearts were seated on their throne
            when they arrived, with a great crowd assembled about them—all sorts
            of little birds and beasts, as well as the whole pack of cards: the
            Knave was standing before them, in chains, with a soldier on each
            side to guard him; and near the King was the White Rabbit, with a
            trumpet in one hand, and a scroll of parchment in the other. In the
            very middle of the court was a table, with a large dish of tarts
            upon it: they looked so good, that it made Alice quite hungry to
            look at them—“I wish they’d get the trial done,” she thought, “and
            hand round the refreshments!” But there seemed to be no chance of
            this, so she began looking at everything about her to pass away the
            time.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice had never been in a court of justice before, but she had read
            about them in books, and she was quite pleased to find that she knew
            the name of nearly everything there. “That’s the judge,” she said to
            herself, “because of his great wig.”
        }

        \Para {
            The judge, by the way, was the King, and as he wore his crown over
            the wig, (look at the frontispiece if you want to see how he did
            it,) he did not look at all comfortable, and it was certainly not
            becoming.
        }

        \Para {
            “And that’s the jury\=box,” thought Alice, “and those twelve
            creatures,” (she was obliged to say “creatures,” you see, because
            some of them were animals, and some were birds,) “I suppose they are
            the jurors.” She said this last word two or three times over to
            herself, being rather proud of it: for she thought, and rightly too,
            that very few little girls of her age knew the meaning of it at all.
            However, “jury-men” would have done just as well.
        }

        \Para {
            The twelve jurors were all writing very busily on slates. “What are
            they doing?” Alice whispered to the Gryphon. “They can’t have
            anything to put down yet, before the trial’s begun.”
        }

        \Para {
            “They’re putting down their names,” the Gryphon whispered in reply,
            “for fear they should forget them before the end of the trial.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Stupid things!” Alice began in a loud indignant voice, but she
            stopped herself hastily, for the White Rabbit cried out, “Silence in
            the court!” and the King put on his spectacles and looked anxiously
            round, to make out who was talking.
        }

        \Para {
            Alice could see, as well as if she were look\-ing over their
            shoulders, that all the jurors were writing down “stupid things!” on
            their slates, and she could even make out that one of them didn’t
            know how to spell “stupid,” and that he had to ask his neighbour to
            tell him. “A nice muddle their slates’ll be in before the trial’s
            over!” thought Alice.
        }

        \Para {
            One of the jurors had a pencil that squeaked. This, of
            course, Alice could \Italic{not} stand, and she went round the court and got
            behind him, and very soon found an opportunity of taking it away.
            She did it so quickly that the poor little juror (it was Bill, the
            Lizard) could not make out at all what had become of it; so, after
            hunting all about for it, he was obliged to write with one finger
            for the rest of the day; and this was of very little use, as it left
            no mark on the slate.
        }

        \Para {
            “Herald, read the accusation!” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            On this the White Rabbit blew three blasts on the trumpet, and then
            unrolled the parch\-ment scroll, and read as follows:—
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 166 (Verso): Top Plate #37 (White Rabbit Herald) + Verse + 2 lines
        // ============================================================
        \TopPlate(src: "images/tenniel_37.png", grid_lines: 15.5, img_top: 20pt)

        \PoemBlock(size: 10pt, leading: 15.5pt, italic: true) {
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 26pt, indent: 0pt) { “The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 26pt, indent: 14.5pt) { All on a summer day: }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 26pt, indent: 0pt) { The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts, }
            \VerseLine(left_margin: 26pt, indent: 14.5pt) { And took them quite away!” }
            \Spacer(height: 12pt)
        }

        \Para {
            “Consider your verdict,” the King said to the jury.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 167–169: Calling the First Witness (The Hatter)
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “Not yet, not yet!” the Rabbit hastily in\-terrupted. “There’s a great
            deal to come before that!”
        }

        \Para {
            “Call the first witness,” said the King; and the White Rabbit blew
            three blasts on the trumpet, and called out, “First witness!”
        }

        \Para {
            The first witness was the Hatter. He came in with a teacup in one
            hand, and a piece of bread\=and\=butter in the other. “I beg pardon,
            your Majesty,” he began, “for bringing these in: but I hadn’t quite
            finished my tea when I was sent for.”
        }

        \Para {
            “You ought to have finished,” said the King. “When did you begin?”
        }

        \Para {
            The Hatter looked at the March Hare, who had followed him into the
            court, arm\=in\=arm with the Dormouse. “Fourteenth of March, I
            \Italic{think} it was,” he said.
        }

        \Para {
            “Fifteenth,” said the March Hare.
        }

        \Para {
            “Sixteenth,” added the Dormouse.
        }

        \Para {
            “Write that down,” the King said to the jury, and the jury eagerly
            wrote down all three dates on their slates, and then added them up,
            and reduced the answer to shillings and pence.
        }

        \Para {
            “Take off your hat,” the King said to the Hatter.
        }

        \Para {
            “It isn’t mine,” said the Hatter.
        }

        \Para {
            “\Italic{Stolen!}” the King exclaimed, turning to the jury, who
            instantly made a memorandum of the fact.
        }

        \Para {
            “I keep them to sell,” the Hatter added as an
            explanation: “I’ve none of my own. I’m a hatter.”
        }

        \Para {
            Here the Queen put on her spectacles, and began staring hard
            at the Hatter, who turned pale and fidgeted.
        }

        \Para {
            “Give your evidence,” said the King; “and don’t be nervous, or I’ll
            have you executed on the spot.”
        }

        \Para {
            This did not seem to encourage the witness at all: he kept shifting
            from one foot to the other, looking uneasily at the Queen, and in
            his confusion he bit a large piece out of his teacup instead of the
            bread\=and\=butter.
        }

        \Para {
            Just at this moment Alice felt a very curious sensation, which
            puzzled her a good deal until she made out what it was: she was
            beginning to grow larger again, and she thought at first she would
            get up and leave the court; but on second thoughts she decided to
            remain where she was as long as there was room for her.
        }

        \Para {
            “I wish you wouldn’t squeeze so,” said the Dormouse, who was sitting
            next to her. “I can hardly breathe.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I can’t help it,” said Alice very meekly: “I’m growing.”
        }

        \Para {
            “You’ve no right to grow \Italic{here,}” said the Dormouse.
        }

        \Para {
            “Don’t talk nonsense,” said Alice more boldly: “you know you’re
            growing too.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Yes, but \Italic{I} grow at a reasonable pace,” said the Dormouse:
            “not in that ridiculous fashion.” And he got up very sulkily and
            crossed over to the other side of the court.
        }

        \Para(align: Align.JustifyAll) {
            All this time the Queen had never left off staring at the Hatter,
            and, just as the Dormouse
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 170 (Verso): Left Inset Wrap #38 (Trembling Hatter)
        // ============================================================
        \FlushPara(
            align: Align.JustifyAll,
            pagination: \Pagination(breakable: false, break_before: Break.Page, keep_with_next: true)
        ) {
            crossed the court, she said to one of the officers of the court,
            “Bring me the list of the singers in the last concert!” on which the
            wretched Hatter
        }

        \WrapLeftStack(
            src: "images/tenniel_38.png",
            img_offset_x: -3pt,
            img_offset_y: 12pt,
            narrow_x: 151pt
        ) {
            \FlushPara {
                trembled so, that he shook both his shoes off.
            }
            \Para {
                “Give your evi\-dence,” the King re\-peated an\-grily, “or I’ll have
                you execu\-ted, wheth\-er you’re ner\-vous or not.”
            }
            \Para(align: Align.JustifyAll) {
                “I’m a poor man, your Maj\-esty,” the Hatter began
                in a trem\-bling voice,
            }
        }

        \FlushPara {
            “and I hadn’t but just begun my tea—not above a week or so—and what
            with the bread-and\=butter get\-ting so thin—and the twin\-kling of the
            \NoBreak{tea\DoubleEmDash{}”}
        }

        \Para {
            “The twinkling of \Italic{what?}” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            “It \Italic{began} with the tea,” the Hatter replied.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 171–172: Twinkling & Suppressing the Guinea-Pigs
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “Of course twinkling begins with a T!” said the King sharply. “Do
            you take me for a dunce? Go on!”
        }

        \Para {
            “I’m a poor man,” the Hatter went on, “and most things twinkled
            after that—only the March Hare said\DoubleEmDash{}”
        }

        \Para {
            “I didn’t!” the March Hare interrupted in a great hurry.
        }

        \Para {
            “You did!” said the Hatter.
        }

        \Para {
            “I deny it!” said the March Hare.
        }

        \Para {
            “He denies it,” said the King: “leave out that part.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, at any rate, the Dormouse said—” the Hatter went on, looking
            anxiously round to see if he would deny it too: but the Dormouse
            denied nothing, being fast asleep.
        }

        \Para {
            “After that,” continued the Hatter, “I cut some more
            bread\=and\=butter\DoubleEmDash{}”
        }

        \Para {
            “But what did the Dormouse say?” one of the jury asked.
        }

        \Para {
            “That I can’t remember,” said the Hatter.
        }

        \Para {
            “You \Italic{must} remember,” remarked the King, “or I’ll have you
            executed.”
        }

        \Para {
            The miserable Hatter dropped his teacup and bread\=and\=butter, and
            went down on one knee. “I’m a poor man, your Majesty,” he began.
        }

        \Para {
            “You’re a \Italic{very} poor \Italic{speaker,}” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            Here one of the guinea\=pigs cheered, and was immediately suppressed
            by the officers of the court. (As that is rather a hard word, I will
            just explain to you how it was done. They had a large canvass bag,
            which tied up at the mouth with strings: into this they slipped the
            guinea-pig, head first, and then sat upon it.)
        }

        \Para {
            “I’m glad I’ve seen that done,” thought Alice. “I’ve so often read
            in the newspapers, at the end of trials, ‘There was some attempt at
            applause, which was immediately suppressed by the officers of the
            court,’ and I never under\-stood what it meant till now.”
        }

        \Para {
            “If that’s all you know about it, you may stand down,” continued the
            King.
        }

        \Para(align: Align.JustifyAll) {
            “I can’t go no lower,” said the Hatter: “I’m
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Page 173 (Recto): 4 lines + Mid Plate #39 (Fleeing Hatter) + 8 lines
        // ============================================================
        \FlushPara(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: false, break_before: Break.Page, keep_with_next: true)) {
            on the floor, as it is.”
        }

        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: false, keep_with_next: true)) {
            “Then you may \Italic{sit} down,” the King replied.
        }

        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: false, keep_with_next: true)) {
            Here the other guinea\=pig cheered, and was suppressed.
        }

        \MidPlate(src: "images/tenniel_39.png", grid_lines: 10.0, img_top: 12pt)

        \Para {
            “Come, that finishes the guinea\=pigs!” thought Alice. “Now we shall
            get on better.”
        }

        \Para {
            “I’d rather finish my tea,” said the Hatter, with an anxious look at
            the Queen, who was reading the list of singers.
        }

        \Para {
            “You may go,” said the King, and the Hatter hurriedly left the
            court, without even waiting to put his shoes on.
        }

        // ============================================================
        // Pages 174–175: The Duchess's Cook & calling "Alice!"
        // ============================================================
        \Para(pagination: \Pagination(breakable: true, break_before: Break.Page)) {
            “\DoubleEmDash{}and just take his head off outside,” the Queen added to one of
            the officers; but the Hatter was out of sight before the officer
            could get to the door.
        }

        \Para {
            “Call the next witness!” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            The next witness was the Duchess’ cook. She carried the pepper\=box
            in her hand, and Alice guessed who it was, even before she got into
            the court, by the way the people near the door began sneezing all at
            once.
        }

        \Para {
            “Give your evidence,” said the King.
        }

        \Para {
            “Shan’t,” said the cook.
        }

        \Para {
            The King looked anxiously at the White Rabbit, who said in a low
            voice, “Your Majesty must cross\=examine \Italic{this} witness.”
        }

        \Para {
            “Well, if I must, I must,” the King said with a melancholy air, and,
            after folding his arms and frowning at the cook till his eyes were
            nearly out of sight, he said in a deep voice, “What are tarts made
            of?”
        }

        \Para {
            “Pepper, mostly,” said the cook.
        }

        \Para {
            “Treacle,” said a sleepy voice behind her.
        }

        \Para {
            “Collar that Dormouse!” the Queen shrieked out. “Behead that
            Dormouse! Turn that Dor\-mouse out of court! Suppress him! Pinch him!
            Off with his whiskers!”
        }

        \Para {
            For some minutes the whole court was in confusion, getting the
            Dormouse turned out, and, by the time they had settled down again,
            the cook had disappeared.
        }

        \Para {
            “Never mind!” said the King, with an air of great relief. “Call the
            next witness.” And he added in an under\=tone to the Queen, “Really,
            my dear, \Italic{you} must cross\=examine the next witness. It quite
            makes my forehead ache!”
        }

        \Para {
            Alice watched the White Rabbit as he fumbled over the list, feeling
            very curious to see what the next witness would be like, “—for they
            haven’t got much evidence \Italic{yet,}” she said to herself.
            Imagine her surprise, when the White Rabbit read out, at the top of
            his shrill little voice, the name “Alice!”
        }

        \ChapterEnd()
    };

    \alias right: Distance = x + width;
    \alias height: Distance = ch.height;
    \alias bottom: Distance = y + height;
}
